  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 124 AM EDT Tue Jul 21 2026\n\nThe bulk of this discussion will focus on the today through \ntomorrow timeframe. There is a high level of uncertainty regarding\nthe timing and potential impacts during this timeframe, as there \nare various model solutions. Generally though, we are looking at \nthe possibility of several rounds of showers and storms with the \npotential of strong to severe storms from later this morning \nthrough tomorrow morning.\n\nTropical Storm Bertha became officially named as of last night. Even \nthough she will remain well to our south in the Gulf, her presence \nand slow movement westward may impact how things move and progress \nfor today into tomorrow. From her current location off of the FL \nPeninsula coast, she will be located near New Orleans tomorrow \nmorning. Part of the reason for her movement is because of weak \nflow/steering over much of the southern U.S.. The highest heights of \nthe upper ridge will sink towards Texas with time, aiding Bertha to \nsomewhere in the western Gulf later this week.\n\nThe pattern of high amplitude ridging to the west and troughing with \nshortwave impulses to the east continues. Multiple shortwave \nimpulses will round the bottom of the trough before it's carried \ninto far NE Canada later tomorrow into Thursday. At the surface, \na cold front currently draped over IL to IA to NE/KS will \neventually shift our direction, impacting us well before it even \narrives. The actual front itself may not arrive until early \ntomorrow.\n\nThe potential impacts from today may come from at least three \nseparate rounds of showers and thunderstorms. Each round may become \nstronger as the front gets closer and we introduce higher shear. \nBecause of this possibility, it may impact the severity and \nevolution of the next round, so uncertainty is on the lower side. \nRuns of recent CAMs cannot totally grasp it or even try to align \nwith one another. In the next couple of hours or so, some isolated \nto scattered showers and possible thunderstorms may develop across \nour northern tier. They will most likely mimic what we've seen the \npast couple of days with high PWATs and low overall shear. There may \nbe a period of drying or there could be continuing activity until \nthe next round.\n\nActivity for the most part will be shifting down from the north and \nnorthwest. Later this afternoon and evening could introduce the next \nround where we will be at peak heating. Everything up until this \npoint is pre-frontal. The main threats from storms will be damaging \nwind gusts and potential flooding issues. After dark, or the next \nround, will confidently be the period with the least confidence and \ncertainty. Better shear rolls in as well as SRH, but surface \ninstability falls off some after peak heating. CAMs disagree on \ntiming of arrival as well, which Bertha may be partly to blame. \nDamaging wind gusts will continue to be the greatest threat with a\ngeneral linear storm mode. If we can generate hail, it will most \nlikely be of the smaller variety. A low-end tornado threat does \nexist, mainly for the northern plateau into southwest VA. Unless \nthere's some localized effects, backed surface winds may be hard \nto accomplish.\n\nThe \"third\" round may begin as early as 7 PM EDT, persisting through \nthe overnight hours until it weakens. Movement during the overnight, \nat least according to CAMs, doesn't show much southern progression, \nso that puts flooding on the forefront of impacts. Generally, north \nof I-40 and the higher terrain can expect the higher end of QPF.\n\nLater tomorrow into Thursday, when previously thought that it would \ndry out, may keep chances of showers and storms around with the \npotentially stalled front to our south, Bertha just off of the LA \ncoast, and high pressure desperately trying to barrel in from the \nnorth. WPC recognizes this, keeping MRGL to SLGT ERO tomorrow into \nThursday.\n\nThe weekend as far as precipitation goes, does become more uncertain \nwith a shortwave approaching from the northwest while the front may \nstill be hung up to our south. We can say with certainty though, \nthat temperatures tomorrow and into the first part of the weekend \nwill be noticeably below normal with low and mid 80s. Temperatures \ngradually warm to near normal values early next week.",
